    code: { lang: "javascript", title: "objects.js — complete, runnable (Node / browser console)", code: "const event = { user: \"rahim\", port: 23, status: \"fail\" };\nconst { user, status } = event;                    // destructuring\nconsole.log(\"user:\", user, \"| status:\", status);\nconsole.log(\"keys:\", Object.keys(event).join(\",\"));\nconst merged = { ...event, reviewed: true };       // spread copy + add\nconsole.log(\"merged port:\", merged.port, \"| reviewed:\", merged.reviewed);\nconst text = JSON.stringify(event);\nconsole.log(\"json:\", text);\nconst back = JSON.parse(text);\nconsole.log(\"round trip equal:\", back.user === event.user && back.port === event.port);\nconsole.log(\"missing key:\", event.owner ?? \"unassigned\");", output: "user: rahim | status: fail\nkeys: user,port,status\nmerged port: 23 | reviewed: true\njson: {\"user\":\"rahim\",\"port\":23,\"status\":\"fail\"}\nround trip equal: true\nmissing key: unassigned", lines: ["Destructuring pulled exactly two fields by NAME — order in the object does not matter, the names do.", "merged is a new object: event has no 'reviewed' key (check with Object.keys(event) — unchanged).", "The JSON text is byte-for-byte what a Python json.dumps would produce — the format is language-neutral.", "event.owner is undefined, not an error — ?? converted the absence into a stated default."] },
    mistakes: ["Mutating a shared object (event.reviewed = true) when a spread copy was promised — state bugs downstream.", "Assuming JSON keeps everything: functions, undefined, and Dates (become strings) do not survive.", "Deep access without ?. on data you do not control — a missing level throws TypeError."],
    security: "API responses are untrusted objects: destructure the fields you expect, default the optional ones with ??, and never merge user objects into trusted ones without a key allowlist (prototype pollution lives in careless merges).",
