    code: { lang: "bash", title: "text.sh — complete, runnable (creates its own sample log)", code: "#!/usr/bin/env bash\nprintf \"2026-11-15 09:41 fail rahim 203.0.113.44\\n2026-11-15 09:42 ok karim 198.51.100.7\\n2026-11-15 09:43 fail rahim 203.0.113.44\\n2026-11-15 09:44 fail admin 203.0.113.44\\n\" > app.log\necho \"fail users:\"\ngrep ' fail ' app.log | cut -d' ' -f4 | sort | uniq -c\necho \"third column of line 1: $(head -1 app.log | awk '{print $3}')\"\necho \"fail count by awk: $(awk '/ fail / {c++} END {print c}' app.log)\"\nsed 's/203.0.113.44/<ip>/' app.log | head -1\necho \"unique ips: $(awk '{print $5}' app.log | sort -u | wc -l)\"", output: "fail users:\n      1 admin\n      2 rahim\nthird column of line 1: fail\nfail count by awk: 3\n2026-11-15 09:41 fail rahim <ip>\nunique ips: 2", lines: ["cut -f4 took the user field (date, time, status, USER, ip) — field numbers are a contract with the log format; a format change renumbers everything.", "awk counted the same 3 fails WITHOUT a pipeline: pattern '/ fail /' plus a counter plus END — one tool doing grep+wc's job.", "sed masked only the FIRST line (head -1) and only the first occurrence per line — /g would mask all occurrences.", "sort -u counted 2 unique IPs: 203.0.113.44 (three times) and 198.51.100.7 — deduplication as a tally step."] },
    mistakes: ["cut with the wrong delimiter assumption on real logs (multiple spaces, tabs) — awk's whitespace handling is sturdier.", "sed without /g when every occurrence needed masking — a half-masked log shared is a leak.", "grep patterns with unescaped dots (203.0 matches 203x0 too) — quote and escape for literal IPs."],
    security: "Masking before sharing is a data-protection control: sed pipelines that replace IPs/users/tokens are how logs leave the building safely. And grep patterns from user input are regex injection — quote and use -F (fixed strings) for literal searches.",
    pc: ["Run text.sh; add a fifth line (fail karim from the second IP) and predict every changed count.", "Mask BOTH the IP and the user field with two sed expressions in one pipeline."],
    android: ["Android: Termux ships grep/awk/sed — the same hunt runs on the phone."],
    lab: "Guided lab: in the Playground (or terminal), from the lesson's log produce: fails per IP (tally), and a fully masked copy of the log (IPs and users replaced).",
    mission: "Mission: explain awk's pattern-action-END shape in two sentences.",
